// std.rt: the library's view of the C runtime (stdlib.md 2.10, 3; D13.1,
// D13.2). These are the five entry points of toolchain.md 5.1 that the
// library names, and the list is complete: the module declares no other
// fort_rt_* symbol and nothing that is not one (D11.6). The runtime calls
// behind new, del, panic, assert and the print family are emitted by the
// compiler and never named here (D12). Names are unmangled, so each is the
// C name toolchain.md 5.1 fixes (D9.7); C types map per D9.8, int being i32
// and size_t u64. stdlib.md 3 owns the contract of each entry point; the
// lines below say only what a reader of this file needs to call one.

// The element pointer and length of the string@ the runtime built from argv
// at process start, a view of storage the runtime keeps (D11.6, D17.3).
extern fn void* fort_rt_args_ptr();
extern fn u64 fort_rt_args_len();

// Writes out the runtime's buffer for one descriptor, if it has one (D11.5).
extern fn void fort_rt_flush(i32 fd);

// Writes out every runtime buffer (D11.5); declared for programs that write
// through libc.write after printing, since the library never calls it.
extern fn void fort_rt_flush_all();

// Flushes every runtime buffer and exits with status & 0xFF (D11.6).
extern fn noreturn fort_rt_exit(i32 status);
